<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Commune of Asti</h1><p class="st-meta">1095 CE – 1342 CE (approx.) · capital Asti</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Piedmontese city-republic whose merchants and money-lenders spread across France and Flanders, giving the 'Lombard' bankers their name in the north.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Asti had long been ruled by its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, but consuls representing the citizens are recorded in 1095, making it one of the earliest communes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>. It grew rich on the trade route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, the Alpine passes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and resisted both its bishop and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/march-of-montferrat">March of Montferrat</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The commune was run by consuls and later a podestà, with powerful merchant families building tower houses in the city. Asti's financiers set up pawn and loan banks across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-savoy">Savoy</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> burned the city in 1155, but it recovered and won a wide rural territory. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palio-of-asti">Palio of Asti</a> horse race is first recorded in 1275.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Faction strife between the Solaro and de Castello families led the city to submit to outside lords, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Angevins</a>, and in 1342 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luchino-visconti-lord-of-milan">Luchino Visconti</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-milan">Lordship of Milan</a>. Asti later passed as a dowry to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> and then to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/commune-of-asti.jpg" alt="Commune of Asti" width="320" height="541"><figcaption>Image: Neq00 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asti_collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1095 CE – 1342 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Asti</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Piedmontese, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Asti, Asti</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1218&amp;focus=commune-of-asti" aria-label="Commune of Asti on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4876 591 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Commune of Asti lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5196 847L5218 839M5196 847L5218 830M5196 847L5140 827"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5218 839h0M5218 830h0M5140 827h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5196 847h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5196 847h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Asti. <a href="/atlas?year=1218&amp;focus=commune-of-asti">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
